Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:02.380
80%.

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.780
That's how similar our protein coding genes are to those of mice.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.360
Humans are larger,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.370
smarter and live longer than mice,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.030
not to mention the fact that humans don't have fur or a tail.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.140
The reason we have been able to calculate our similarity to mice is because of a massive 

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.850
effort to sequence the genomes of humans,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.150
an undertaking called the Human Genome Project,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.350
as well as from sequencing the genomes of mice and many other organisms.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.560
Genome sequencing involves reading through the A's, T's,

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.470
G's and C's that make up our DNA,

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:39.840
and it has given us a lot of information about what our genes are and how they are 

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.600
organized.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.840
And it has helped us improve how we diagnose and even treat human disease.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.370
But genome sequencing involves generating very large sets of data,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.720
so we need powerful tools to decipher all those ATGC's.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.540
This is where the rapidly growing field bioinformatics comes in.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.920
Extremely powerful computers are being used to store and manipulate all of this 

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:04.360
data,

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.850
and the people behind the computers are bioinformaticians, scientists who are often 

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.360
trained both in biology as well as math or computer science.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.340
These multidisciplinary researchers develop methods and software tools to 

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.350
program computers to dig through and make sense of all of this data.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.810
So how did bioinformatics help us learn that humans and mice have 80% 

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.710
similarity in their protein coding genes? One way bioinformaticians approach this type 

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.440
of question is by looking for small sequences in one genome that match the other 

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:38.850
genome.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.930
This is like doing a word search on your computer.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.390
But instead of searching for the word Shakespeare in your English term paper,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:47.880
you scan for ATTGCACGTCTA.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.970
Once matching areas are found, researchers design algorithms that can scan past 

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:01.870
the ends of both sequences to see just how far the matching regions extend.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.460
So in this case,

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:08.230
do the letters after the CTA continue to match between the mouse and 

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.300
human sequences?

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.270
If they do not and a difference is present,

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.940
we can analyze the following sequence and start to figure out which genes are 

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.670
involved in many of the traits that make us humans different from mice, like brain 

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.350
development and longevity.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.050
The flip side of this is that we can also find sequences of the genome that are 

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.360
highly similar across different species.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.740
In addition to sharing parts of our genome with mice,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.150
humans have genes in common with plants,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.180
flies, and even microscopic bacteria.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.470
Thes regions are called conserved genes,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.180
and because their shared across many species,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.650
they likely code for proteins that are essential for life on earth.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.080
In addition to finding the similarities and differences between the genomes of different organisms,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.000
sequencing technology has also allowed us to pick up differences in DNA between different 

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:03.480
people.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.060
This has been particularly important because it helps us better understand human 

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:08.630
disease.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.950
Let's say you knew a specific DNA base in a gene was different from person to person,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.170
and you wanted to see if that difference was important for a disease, like diabetes.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.020
You could sequence that base in 50 healthy people and 50 people with 

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:23.850
diabetes.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.020
If 47 of 50 people with diabetes had an A, while only 5 out of 

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.880
50 without diabetes did,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.080
that would be strong evidence of association between the A variant and the 

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:36.660
disease.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.090
But importantly,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.950
it does not mean the variant causes the disease.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.100
Researchers have developed technologies to look across hundreds of thousands of 

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.910
sites across the genome for these kinds of single base differences and have 

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.330
looked for correlations between certain bases and disease.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.710
These experiments are known as genome-wide association studies.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.440
Sequencing and bioinformatic analysis are also becoming increasingly 

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.540
important for the diagnosis and treatment of cancer,

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.280
because cancer cells often have many mutations or changes in the 

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.030
nucleotide code compared to a patient's normal cells.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.740
As we learn more and more about the human genome and the cost of sequencing decreases,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.870
doctors can order sequencing of parts of their patients' genomes for a relatively 

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:27.800
low cost.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:32.980
By using algorithms to compare a patient's tumor cells to the normal genome,

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.250
as well as to the tumors of many other patients,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.340
doctors can quickly pinpoint the changes in the DNA that are causing the cancer cells to 

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:41.910
grow uncontrollably.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.480
This helps them choose the best treatment for their patients.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.730
As our ability to sequence genomes continues to increase,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.260
bioinformatics will need to continue to develop faster and more advanced 

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.880
algorithms to handle these massive data sets.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.560
An important part of this field has been the development of large 

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:07.420
centralized databases of genome sequences that can be accessed by 

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.060
anyone.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.580
The challenge for the future will be to continue to grow these databases in a way that 

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.530
helps scientists make important new discoveries while preserving the privacy of 

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:18.960
patients.

